---
import Ironside from "../layouts/Ironside.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import CityFaq from "../components/CityFaq.astro";
import CityServiceSchema from "../components/CityServiceSchema.astro";
import ServiceAreaMap from "../components/ServiceAreaMap.astro";
import { cityBySlug, siblingsOf } from "../data/cities";

const city = cityBySlug("brampton")!;
const nearby = siblingsOf("brampton");

const title = "Epoxy flooring Brampton";
const description =
 "Epoxy and polyaspartic garage floors in Brampton - Springdale, Mount Pleasant, Castlemore, Bramalea. Slabs moisture-tested before we quote. One-day install.";

const faqs = [
 {
 q: "My house in Mount Pleasant is four years old. Is the slab ready to coat?",
 a: "Almost certainly yes. Concrete reaches design strength in about 28 days and finishes releasing construction moisture over the following months, so a four-year-old slab is well past both. Where we do test carefully is anything poured within the last six months, which in a city still building as fast as Brampton is a real scenario. A slab that has been through one full winter is generally good to go.",
 },
 {
 q: "There is a white powdery residue on my garage floor. Does that need dealing with?",
 a: "That is efflorescence - soluble salts carried to the surface as moisture moves up through the slab and evaporates. It is extremely common in Brampton's newer subdivisions because the slabs are young and the water table across much of the city sits fairly high. Brushing it off does not solve it; it will come back. It has to be ground off as part of prep, and its presence tells us to test the slab's moisture properly rather than assume. Coating over efflorescence is a guaranteed bond failure.",
 },
 {
 q: "I have a double garage in Castlemore that is bigger than most. Does that change the price?",
 a: "It changes the day, and therefore the price, but not the rate structure. Castlemore and the estate pockets around Airport Road have some of the largest residential garages we quote anywhere - genuine three-bay floors, occasionally with a separate workshop bay. More area means more grinding and a tighter broadcast window, so those go in the book as full-day jobs with an early start.",
 },
 {
 q: "Bramalea houses are from the 1960s. Are those slabs still good?",
 a: "Usually surprisingly good. Bramalea was built as a planned community and the original pours were generally thick and well finished. What sixty years of use leaves behind is surface wear, oil staining in the centre of the bay, and control joints that have opened. All three are prep problems rather than deal-breakers. The one thing to watch is that some Bramalea garages have been re-surfaced at some point with a thin overlay, and an overlay that is debonding has to come off before anything else happens.",
 },
 {
 q: "Do you work in Brampton often? I have had trouble getting contractors to come out.",
 a: "Yes, and the difficulty you have had is a fair reflection of the market. Brampton has plenty of demand and comparatively few contractors who specialise in floor coatings rather than treating it as a sideline to general flooring or concrete work. We run out from Oakville, about forty minutes via the 407, and we quote in person rather than over the phone from a square-footage guess.",
 },
 {
 q: "Can you do a floor in an industrial unit off Steeles or Airport Road?",
 a: "Yes. Brampton's industrial stock is a good fit for a heavier specification - a thicker build with a quartz broadcast rather than decorative flake, and line marking where the floor is a working surface. Those are usually scheduled over a weekend so the unit is not out of action, and they need the bay genuinely cleared, not worked around.",
 },
 {
 q: "How does a coating handle a garage that gets used as a workshop?",
 a: "Well, provided the specification matches the use. A decorative flake system in a residential garage is built for cars, bikes and storage. If you are dropping tools, welding, or running a hoist, say so at quote and we will specify a heavier build with a more impact-tolerant topcoat. The failure mode we see on under-specified floors is chipping at the point of impact, and it is avoidable if the conversation happens before the quote rather than after the floor is down.",
 },
];
---

<Ironside title={title} description={description} heroType="dark">
 <CityServiceSchema
 city={city.name}
 citySlug={city.slug}
 wikidata={city.wikidata}
 description={description}
 />

 <section class="hero-dark compact">
 <div class="hero-grad"></div><div class="hero-grain"></div><div class="hero-rim"></div>
 <div class="hero-inner">
 <div class="city-hero-grid">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker mb-2">Epoxy &amp; polyaspartic &middot; Brampton</div>
 <h1 class="h-hero" style="margin-top:12px;max-width:16ch">Epoxy flooring in Brampton.</h1>
 <p class="lede" style="color:var(--color-iron-300);margin-top:24px;max-width:56ch">
 A city of young slabs and very large garages. We grind, we moisture-test before we quote,
 and we deal with the efflorescence properly instead of coating over it.
 </p>
 <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;margin-top:36px;flex-wrap:wrap">
 <a href="#quote" class="btn btn-lg btn-primary">Book a slab assessment</a>
 </div>
 <div class="meta-rule" style="margin-top:36px">
 <span>{city.driveMinutes} min from the Oakville shop via the 407</span><span class="sep"></span>
 <span>Residential and industrial</span>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div id="quote">
 <QuoteForm city={city.name} heading="Free quote in Brampton" sub="We measure the slab, test it for moisture, and quote a fixed price." />
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:end">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">Brampton slabs</div>
 <h2 class="h1 mt-2" style="max-width:22ch">Brampton garage floors: young concrete and big footprints</h2>
 </div>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);max-width:52ch">
 Brampton is unusual in this region: most of its garage floors were poured in the last
 twenty-five years. That makes them flat and structurally sound, and it makes moisture the
 main technical question rather than deterioration.
 </p>
 </div>

 <div class="hood-grid">
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Springdale</h4>
 <p>Dense early-2000s subdivision. Consistent builder slabs, double garages, easy driveway access. About as predictable as this work gets.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Mount Pleasant &amp; Fletcher's Meadow</h4>
 <p>2010s stock, the newest large tranche in the city. Flat slabs, tight lot spacing, and the highest incidence of efflorescence we see anywhere in the region.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Castlemore</h4>
 <p>Estate properties with the largest residential garages we quote - genuine three-bay floors, sometimes with a separate workshop. Full-day jobs.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Credit Valley</h4>
 <p>2000s builder stock on the western edge, backing onto the Credit valley. Straightforward slabs; the main variable is garage size, which ranges widely.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="hood">
 <h4>Bramalea</h4>
 <p>The 1960s planned community. Thick original pours that have aged well, with worn surfaces, oil staining and opened control joints to deal with in prep.</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="split-64" style="align-items:start;gap:56px">
 <div>
 <div class="kicker">The moisture question</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2" style="max-width:24ch">Moisture is the whole job for epoxy flooring in Brampton</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px;max-width:60ch">
 Every coating failure has one of two causes: bad prep, or moisture. Elsewhere in the
 region the age of the housing stock means prep dominates. In Brampton, where so much of
 the stock is recent, moisture is the one that catches people out - and it is the reason
 that white powdery bloom shows up on so many garage floors here.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 Water moving up through a slab carries dissolved salts with it. The water evaporates at
 the surface and the salts stay behind as efflorescence. If you coat over that, you have
 bonded your floor to a layer of loose crystal rather than to concrete, and it will let go - usually in sheets, usually within two winters. The fix is not complicated but it is not
 optional: grind it off, test what the slab is actually doing, and where the reading is
 high, put down a moisture-mitigating primer before the build coats.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px;max-width:60ch">
 This is also the honest reason we will occasionally tell a Brampton homeowner to wait. A
 slab poured a few months ago in a house you have just moved into may simply not be ready.
 Waiting one season costs nothing. Coating early costs the whole floor.
 </p>
 </div>
 <div>
 <ServiceAreaMap city={city.name} lat={city.lat} lng={city.lng} span={0.13} />
 <div style="margin-top:24px">
 <div class="kicker">Areas we cover</div>
 <div class="nearby">
 {city.neighbourhoods.map((n) => <a href="#quote">{n}</a>)}
 <a href="#quote">Sandalwood</a>
 <a href="#quote">Heart Lake</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Large garages</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Coating a three-bay Brampton garage floor</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Brampton has a lot of very large residential garages, particularly in Castlemore and the
 estate pockets, and floor area changes the install more than people expect. Polyaspartic has
 a working window measured in minutes once the parts are mixed. On a small single bay that is
 generous. On a nine-hundred-square-foot three-bay floor it means the crew has to be sized to
 the area, the mixing has to be staged, and the broadcast has to move continuously.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 The practical consequence for you is that big floors are booked as dedicated full days with
 an early start, not squeezed in behind another job. If a contractor quotes a three-bay
 Castlemore garage as a half-day, that is worth asking about - it usually means either a
 thinner build than you are expecting, or a plan to run out of working time and rush the
 finish at the far end.
 </p>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/polyaspartic-garage-floors">How polyaspartic differs from epoxy</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost">What a floor costs</a>
 <a href="/epoxy-flooring">All service areas</a>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm" style="border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Working through the seasons</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">Brampton epoxy flooring runs on the slab&rsquo;s calendar, not the weather</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 In most of our markets the seasonal question is about ambient temperature and humidity on
 install day. In Brampton it is more often about how old the concrete is, because so much of
 the city's housing stock is recent enough that the slab's own drying curve is the binding
 constraint.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 A slab poured in late autumn will sit through winter in a cold garage, and cold concrete
 dries slowly. The same slab poured in May will be materially drier by September. That means
 two identical houses on the same Mount Pleasant street, finished six months apart, can give
 very different readings on the same day - and it is why we will not quote a recent build off
 a photograph.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 For anything genuinely new, the practical guidance is to plan the coating for the season
 after the slab's first full winter. By then the concrete has been through a heating cycle,
 the bulk of the construction moisture has left, and any shrinkage cracking has already
 happened, which means we can chase and fill it once rather than watching a new crack open
 through a fresh floor.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Older Bramalea slabs have none of this problem. Sixty-year-old concrete is as dry as it is
 going to get, so those jobs run to a normal schedule year-round, and polyaspartic makes them
 viable through the winter when the crews have more room in the book.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <section class="section section-sm" style="background:var(--color-iron-50);border-top:1px solid var(--color-iron-100);border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-iron-100)">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Before you sign anything</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">The one question to ask a Brampton epoxy flooring contractor</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Ask what happens if the moisture test comes back high - and listen carefully to the answer.
 In a city this full of young slabs it is the question that separates a contractor who
 understands the local stock from one who is applying a generic process.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 There are only three honest answers. Install a moisture-mitigating primer, which costs real
 money and should be quoted as a contingency rather than sprung on you on the day. Wait until
 the slab has dried, which costs you nothing but time. Or decline the job. Anyone who tells
 you the reading does not matter, or that their product "breathes", is describing a failure
 you will be looking at in two years.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Two further checks are worth making here specifically. First, ask how they intend to deal
 with efflorescence - the correct answer involves a grinder, not a stiff brush and a rinse.
 Second, if you have a three-bay garage, ask how many people will be on site. A large floor
 with a two-person crew means the far end is being finished outside the material's working
 window, and that shows up as a visible difference in texture across the bay.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 None of this is exotic. It is simply that Brampton's garages are newer and bigger than the
 regional average, and both of those facts change what a good job looks like.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 One last thing worth knowing, because it comes up constantly in the newer subdivisions: a
 builder's garage slab is finished to a different standard than the basement or the driveway.
 It is poured quickly, floated rather than power-trowelled in many cases, and it is the last
 thing anybody inspects before closing. That is not a defect and it is not worth arguing with
 the builder about, but it does mean the surface is often more open and more absorbent than
 homeowners expect. Practically, an open surface grinds well and takes a coating happily. It
 also means the slab has been drinking in whatever has been spilled on it since the day the
 house was handed over, so if there is an oil patch under where the car has been parked for
 three years, tell us at quote. Contaminated concrete has to be ground past rather than
 sealed over, and knowing about it in advance is the difference between a planned extra hour
 and an unwelcome surprise halfway through the morning.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>


 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container-narrow">
 <div class="kicker">Install day</div>
 <h2 class="h2 mt-2">How a Brampton epoxy floor install actually runs</h2>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:20px">
 Brampton days start early because the drive is forty minutes and the floors are large. The
 crew is sized to the garage rather than to a standard template - a Castlemore three-bay gets
 more people than a Springdale double, because polyaspartic's working window does not stretch
 to accommodate a short-handed crew.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Masking and dust control first, then the grind. On newer slabs the grind doubles as the
 efflorescence removal step, which is why we will not simply rinse a floor and call it
 prepared. Once the surface is profiled and vacuumed we re-check moisture. On a slab under
 about two years old that reading decides what happens next - straight to basecoat, or a
 mitigating primer first.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 Basecoat, flake broadcast to refusal, scrape-back, topcoat. On a large floor the broadcast is
 staged so the crew is never chasing material that has started to set, which is the single
 most visible difference between a well-run big install and a rushed one. Walkable that
 evening, vehicles the next morning.
 </p>
 <p class="p" style="color:var(--fg2);margin-top:16px">
 The same sequence runs in <a href="/epoxy-flooring-vaughan">Vaughan</a> and
 <a href="/epoxy-flooring-toronto">Toronto</a>. More on
 <a href="/about">how we work</a>, and the full
 <a href="/service-areas">service-area map</a>.
 </p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <CityFaq faqs={faqs} city={city.name} />

 <section class="section section-sm">
 <div class="container">
 <div class="kicker">Nearby</div>
 <h2 class="h3 mt-2">Epoxy flooring in nearby cities</h2>
 <div class="nearby">
 <a href="/service-areas/oakville">Oakville</a>
 {nearby.map((c) => <a href={`/epoxy-flooring-${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a>)}
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>
</Ironside>
